WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.180
Career and Technical Education, or CTE,

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.940
may be the most popular notion in school reform today.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.280
But for CTE to be more than another fad,

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.740
schools need to make some difficult choices
about exactly how it will work.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:23.220
My friend, and former state superintendent, Tony Bennett, recently flagged three key questions

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.320
about CTE that need to be resolved.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.620
One: Will we accept that expanding CTE may
eliminate some traditional teaching positions?

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:35.140
Many CTE students spend part of the school day learning workforce skills, which means

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.873
schools may not need as many teachers.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.580
Two: Will we accept that traditional teacher preparation is inadequate for CTE instructors?

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:46.060
Schools of education don't prepare graduates to teach

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.120
skill-based occupations such as welding or firefighting.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:57.180
Three: Will we accept CTE's reliance on high-stakes testing? Many CTE students must pass a national,

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.620
state, or industry-based assessment to be
certified in their chosen field.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:07.500
For CTE to deliver on its promise, schools 
have to confront some tough decisions.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.740
What do you think of Career and Technical
Education? Let us know in your comments.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.680
Also, let us know what other topics you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:23.600
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

